While General Matrix-Matrix Multiplication (GEMM) often nearly approaches the peak performance of modern GPUs, other fundamental dense linear algebra algorithms, such as Cholesky factorization, exhibit a significant performance gap. We identify the root cause of this inefficiency as the conventional ‘delayed update’ paradigm, a multi-kernel approach that relies on coarse-grained synchronization and incurs substantial data movement overhead, particularly for small-to-medium matrices. This paper challenges that paradigm by introducing a Non-Delayed Cholesky Factorization. Our core insight is that the tile-level dataflow of the Cholesky trailing update is structurally isomorphic to that of a high-performance GEMM. We exploit this by redesigning the entire factorization to execute like a GEMM within a single, persistent CUDA kernel. In our tile-centric model, each thread block assumes lifetime ownership of a single matrix tile, performing all updates and the final factorization in-place using on-chip memory. A lightweight, asynchronous dependency-tracking mechanism coordinates thousands of blocks without global barriers, largely increasing concurrency. We evaluate our design on diverse hardware platforms, including three generations of NVIDIA GPUs (A100, RTX 5090, and H100) and an AMD GPU (R9700). Our non-delayed Cholesky factorization consistently outperforms state-of-the-art libraries, achieving speedups of up to 2.28 × over cuSOLVER and 2.16 × over rocSOLVER. More profoundly, we demonstrate that our algorithm’s structural efficiency is its key advantage: it consistently converts over 90% of its underlying GEMM kernel’s performance into final factorization throughput across all architectures. This stands in stark contrast to cuSOLVER, whose efficiency relative to cuBLAS fluctuates significantly (from >90% down to 60%) across different GPUs. By importing the execution philosophy of GEMM, rather than merely calling it as a subroutine, our work presents a new and highly effective model for designing dense Cholesky factorizations on modern accelerators.